# The scanner the semgrep job in ci.yml installs, pinned, in a file Dependabot
# reads.
#
# A version written inside a workflow step is watched by nothing: Dependabot
# reads go.mod for Go modules and `uses:` for actions, and it does not read
# shell commands. A requirements file is an ecosystem it does read, so a new
# semgrep release arrives as a pull request against this file.
#
# Pinned rather than floating, because an unpinned analyser turns somebody
# else's release into a red build on a commit that changed nothing.
#
# LGPL-2.1-or-later, read from the wheel metadata of 1.185.0 on PyPI on
# 2026-11-23. It reads the tree in CI and is never part of anything this
# project ships, so its licence is recorded rather than sieved.
#
# This is the one line a person writes. requirements-semgrep.txt beside it is
# every package that line installs, pinned, compiled from this file by
# pip-compile rather than written by hand. With this line alone a
# vulnerability scanner would have to guess what pip picks. And with an .in
# file present Dependabot runs pip-compile itself, so what it proposes is a set
# pip can install rather than every line raised on its own.
#
# Every package is pinned to a version AND to the bytes of its files, and the
# job installs with --require-hashes and wheels only, the way every pip install
# in these workflows does - building a source archive runs its setup code.
#
# To compile it by hand, from the root of the repository, with the Python the
# job uses:
#
#   docker run --rm -v "$PWD/.github:/w" -w /w python:3.14-slim sh -c "pip install -q pip==26.1.1 pip-tools==7.5.3 && pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras --no-emit-index-url --pip-args '--uploaded-prior-to=<a week ago, for example 2026-11-16T00:00:00Z>' --output-file=requirements-semgrep.txt requirements-semgrep.in"
#
# The date holds back what is younger than a week, the way the cooldown in
# .github/dependabot.yml does. A security fix is the exception, as it is for
# Dependabot.
semgrep==1.185.0
